{"availability":"available","full":"rordenlab/niimath","name":"niimath","description":"niimath - a clone of fslmaths. Try the live demo:","category":"Other","language":"C","stars":84,"forks":26,"created":"2020-01-23T16:20:49Z","updated":"2026-09-29T07:35:19Z","license":null,"branch":"master","homepage":"https://niivue.github.io/niivue-niimath/","topics":[],"demo":"https://niivue.github.io/niivue-niimath/","color":"#323057","ink":"#d6caff","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.512Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.512Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.512Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.512Z","stars":84,"forks":26}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["niimath is an open-source clone of fslmaths, the general image calculator of FSL. It accepts the same commands and gives equivalent results. It also adds operations that fslmaths does not have, such as mesh generation, affine and nonlinear registration, defacing, motion correction, slice-time correction, phase unwrapping and distortion correction.","fslmaths is one of the foundations of the FSL pipelines, such as FEAT, and many other tools call it. This popularity shows that it fills an important role. Scientists are often encouraged to find new solutions, but replication has value too. niimath exists for these reasons:","There is one reason to use fslmaths instead of niimath. niimath is newer and less tested, so unknown corner cases may give poor results. fslmaths has been in use for years. In the few cases where fslmaths differs from its own documentation (described below), you can argue that its result is the correct one, because it agrees with itself. Other tools may have been built to expect that behavior, such as the loss of high frequency signal, and may perform worse when given the documented result."],"features":["fslmaths is free of charge, but it is not open source. This limits its use in other projects, in particular commercial ones.","An open-source license lets niimath use libraries that the FSL team cannot. The release builds use zlib-ng, an accelerated zlib that is much faster than the public domain zlib used by fslmaths. zlib-ng is also fast on…","niimath has minimal dependencies. This makes it easy to distribute, compile and develop. It compiles for macOS, Linux and Windows. FSL cannot target Windows.","niimath was designed from the start for optional parallel processing, with OpenMP and the CloudFlare-enhanced pigz.","Most programs grow organically as needs arise. A clone starts with a full specification, which permits optimization. niimath uses explicit single and double precision pipelines, so the compiler can use the SIMD…"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rordenlab/niimath/blob/master/README.md","fingerprint":"4d08d1b3bda378a328a6211ac31d276d04197ded41bad1c24cea281b018566ee","extractedAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.639Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.639Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"niimath is an open-source clone of fslmaths, the general image calculator of FSL. It accepts the same commands and gives equivalent results. It also adds operations that fslmaths does not have, such as mesh generation, affine and nonlinear registration, defacing, motion correction, slice-time correction, phase unwrapping and distortion correction.","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rordenlab/niimath/blob/master/README.md"},"audience":[],"requirements":[],"fingerprint":"4d08d1b3bda378a328a6211ac31d276d04197ded41bad1c24cea281b018566ee","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rordenlab/niimath/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.640Z"},"overviewRepoUpdated":"2026-09-29T07:35:19Z","readmeSnapshot":{"version":1,"status":"saved","sourceUrl":"https://github.com/rordenlab/niimath/blob/master/README.md","checkedAt":"2026-10-07T20:14:49.644Z","hash":"4d08d1b3bda378a328a6211ac31d276d04197ded41bad1c24cea281b018566ee","originalBytes":75614,"storedBytes":75614,"truncated":false,"searchTerms":["nii